# {{.Name}}

A [Tern](https://github.com/Unfield/Tern) app with accounts, settings and a dashboard built: the starting point of a product, ready to change.

- **Accounts:** sign-up with email verification, password resets, passkeys, two-step sign-in with an authenticator app, and sign-in with GitHub or Google when their keys are set. See [docs/accounts.md](https://github.com/Unfield/Tern/blob/main/docs/accounts.md).
- **Teams:** everyone gets a team at sign-up, invites others by email, and gives each an owner, admin or member role. Users switch between their teams in the sidebar. `/settings/team` has the members, invitations and the team's activity.
- **Billing:** a free plan and a paid one per member, with a trial, through Stripe Checkout and its customer portal. The free plan's limits are checked when creating projects and inviting. Plans are in `internal/app/plans.go`; `/pricing` shows them and `/settings/billing` the team's.
- **Settings:** profile, email address, password, the devices you're signed in on, connected accounts, recent activity, and deleting the account.
- **Projects:** an example of your app's own data, per team, in SQLite with a migration. Replace it with what your app keeps, with a `team_id` on each row.

## Develop

```sh
npm install
npm run dev        # or: go tool tern dev
```

Open <http://localhost:5173> and create an account. Without `SMTP_HOST`, emails aren't sent: their links are in the server's log.

## Build

```sh
npm run build      # or: go tool tern build
./build/{{.Name}}
```

`tern build` produces a single binary with the client embedded. It listens on `PORT` (default 3000) and keeps its data in `app.db` (`DATABASE_PATH`). The Dockerfile builds the same binary into a small image.

## Settings

The app reads them from the environment, and `tern dev` also from a `.env` file: copy `.env.example` to start one. In production, set them in the environment.

| Variable | |
|---|---|
| `BASE_URL` | the app's address, such as `https://example.com`: for links in emails and for passkeys |
| `DATABASE_PATH` | the SQLite database, `app.db` by default |
| `SMTP_HOST`, `SMTP_PORT`, `SMTP_USERNAME`, `SMTP_PASSWORD`, `MAIL_FROM` | send emails through SMTP, in background jobs |
| `GITHUB_CLIENT_ID`, `GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET` | sign-in with GitHub; the callback URL is `<BASE_URL>/auth/github/callback` |
| `GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID`, `GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET` | sign-in with Google; the callback URL is `<BASE_URL>/auth/google/callback` |
| `OAUTH_SECRET` | at least 32 random characters, when GitHub or Google is set |
| `STRIPE_SECRET_KEY`, `STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET` | billing with Stripe: the API key, and the webhook's signing secret. Without the key, every team is on the free plan |
| `STRIPE_PRICE_PRO_MONTHLY`, `STRIPE_PRICE_PRO_YEARLY` | the IDs of the Pro plan's prices in Stripe |
| `TRUSTED_PROXIES` | the addresses of your reverse proxies, comma-separated, so visitors' own addresses are seen |
| `APP_NAME` | the name authenticator apps and passkey prompts show |

## Project layout

```
main.go                      plugins: migrations, jobs, accounts, sign-in with other providers
internal/app/                the database, accounts, email; projects.go is the example data
migrations/                  SQL migrations, applied at start
queries/                     SQL queries; tern generates typed Go for them in queries.gen.go
src/routes/
  +page.svelte               the landing page
  auth.group/                sign in, sign up, two-step code, password reset, email confirmation
  app.group/                 the signed-in app, with the sidebar: its layout requires a user
    dashboard/  projects/  settings/  settings/security/
```

`auth.group` and `app.group` give their pages a layout without adding to their URLs.

## Billing

1. In Stripe's dashboard (test mode first), make a product with a monthly and a yearly price, charged per unit, and set their IDs as `STRIPE_PRICE_PRO_MONTHLY` and `STRIPE_PRICE_PRO_YEARLY`.
2. Set `STRIPE_SECRET_KEY` to the secret key.
3. Forward webhooks to the app: in development, `stripe listen --forward-to localhost:5173/_tern/billing/webhook` prints the secret for `STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET`. In production, add an endpoint at `<BASE_URL>/_tern/billing/webhook` for the `customer.subscription.*`, `checkout.session.completed` and `invoice.*` events.
4. Turn on the customer portal in Stripe's billing settings, so teams can change their plan, update the card and cancel.

Webhooks are processed as background jobs, and each fetches the subscription from Stripe, so a repeated or late event is safe. See [docs/billing.md](https://github.com/Unfield/Tern/blob/main/docs/billing.md).
